Torsion of shafts and the limits of the circular model (@mech-torsion: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @mechanical-engineering, #source "../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY", #source "../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W", #mechanical-engineering "Pressure-loaded-cylinders-shells-and-end-forces.lingua#r_2HT2CN6HMX46P23CA8VRTD7X6T") { r_21X60ESY01KMZF3R8J8HKM9194
**Read:** [Modules in Mechanics of Materials — David Roylance](../sources/Mechanics-of-Materials-Roylance.lingua#r_7K2RYA9504FGFGJTVWHMWXD5WY), Shear and Torsion; [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), linear-elastic solutions and simplified rod theory in chapters 5 and 10.

For a straight circular shaft in elastic torsion, shear stress varies as τ = Tr/J and twist per length is T/(GJ), with J the polar second moment of area. For a solid circle J = πd⁴/32. For a concentric tube subtract the inner diameter contribution. Use the torque history from the dynamics work rather than only the nominal motor rating.

With T = 100 N·m, d = 20 mm, L = 0.5 m and G = 80 GPa, the surface shear is about 63.662 MPa and the twist about 0.03979 rad. Doubling diameter strongly changes both stress and twist because the relevant geometry has fourth-power scaling.

For a noncircular section, warping and the torsional constant replace the simple circular distribution. A geometric polar area moment is not generally the torsional constant. Restrained warping, thin open sections, keyways and shoulders require additional analysis.

**Work:** Derive the circular formula from shear strain and torque equilibrium. Compare solid and hollow shafts at equal mass and equal outer envelope. Distinguish a stiffness requirement from a strength requirement, and identify where stress concentration and cyclic bending will enter the later shaft design.

**Return:** [Mechanical engineering and machine design](../Mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design.lingua#r_0017YZ63HZBZ4P4WD8DECVWE4R).
**Continue:** [Pressure-loaded cylinders, shells and end forces](Pressure-loaded-cylinders-shells-and-end-forces.lingua#r_2HT2CN6HMX46P23CA8VRTD7X6T).

